From f018f7aff8f5e78a3310ee632011989def5d1ca5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AmirSalahY <amir@noury.ch>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 12:12:52 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 41/41] feat(ui): own the command-line palette, and colour
 known subcommands
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Command-line highlighting previously used its own small colour scheme,
distinct from zsh-syntax-highlighting even though the shell integration
disables that plugin inside an owlterm session (the two would otherwise
repaint the same cells). So the line looked different in owlterm than in a
plain shell, and it mislabelled shell keywords: typing `if` or `for` at the
start of a line showed it in red as an invalid command, because the old
classifier only distinguished commands from arguments.

Rewrite the classifier as a small parser that walks the line the way the
plugin's main highlighter does, and colour it with the plugin's own default
styles (read from the installed copy) rather than inventing new ones: quotes,
$-expansions, command and process substitution, redirections, globs, history
expansion, precommands like sudo, and reserved words all get the plugin's
colour and nothing is misclassified as an invalid command anymore. Colours are
sent as terminal palette codes, not fixed hex, so the line follows the user's
theme exactly as it would outside owlterm.

The plugin has no notion of a subcommand — `git status` only colours `git`.
owlterm's completion specs already know a command's real subcommands, so add
one more style on top: a positional word is coloured only if the active spec
(or nested subcommand spec, chained through the line) actually lists it,
otherwise it's left in the terminal's normal colour so a typo stays visible.
Bright green, since it's still a command word, one step lighter than the
green used for the command itself. Spec lookups run through a small
non-blocking cache — the highlighter repaints synchronously on every
keystroke and can't wait on an import; the first keystrokes on a new command
are uncoloured until its spec finishes loading in the background.

Branch names in git commands are no longer coloured on the command line —
the plugin doesn't colour them either, and the one-off lookup that did it
here was the last user of gitRefs.ts's isGitRef, now removed. Branch
suggestions in the dropdown are unaffected.
